  <div class="summary-box">
    <div class="summary-title">🖋 Translator's Summary — Hechsher Kailim 4, Sections 1–12</div>
    <p>
      <strong>Theme:</strong> This halacha is a profound mystical treatise anchored in <em>Likutay Moharan</em> I:66 — the central concept of bringing things <em>from potential into actuality</em> (<em>mekoyach el hapo'al</em>). The Rav Nasan applies this principle across multiple domains simultaneously: the purpose of creation, the rectification of sin, the Omer and Shavuos, immersion in a <em>mikveh</em>, and the preparation of vessels.
    </p>
    <p><strong>The Central Thread — <em>Pi Shnayim</em> (Double Portion):</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>§1–2:</strong> All of creation was brought forth as potential waiting to be actualized by Israel's fulfillment of Torah. Before the Torah was given at Sinai, the world existed in a state of <em>tohu va'vohu</em>. Avraham — and each Jew — is a "creator" in that each mitzvah actualizes another portion of the cosmos.</li>
      <li><strong>§3:</strong> The structure of the letter <span class="heb">אָלֶ"ף</span> symbolizes the bond between the upper spirit (upper <em>yud</em>) and lower spirit (lower <em>yud</em>) via the <em>vav</em>. Sin severs this — <em>"a slanderer separates the aluf."</em> Teshuvah means reconnecting. But since the blemish reaches to a certain level, rectification must come from <em>above</em> that level — hence one needs a true <em>tzadik</em>.</li>
      <li><strong>§4:</strong> Lag BaOmer and the <em>hilula</em> of Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai are explained: the <em>tzadik</em> must ascend to levels unreachable during life in order to rectify the generation — so his passing is a <em>tikkun</em>, not a loss. The Zohar is his bequest to redeem us from exile. The merit of departed tzadikim is the world's essential foundation.</li>
      <li><strong>§5:</strong> To access that legacy, one must shatter obstacles through passionate yearning (<em>chashek</em>). The barley Omer — animal-level feed, associated with mindlessness and obstacles — is waved upward, transforming obstacles into fuel for desire. This is the gateway to receiving the Torah on Shavuos.</li>
      <li><strong>§6:</strong> The <em>sefirah</em> is a re-enactment of the seven days of creation. Each day counted restores and purifies one "creation-day" — so that when Shavuos arrives, all seven days stand rectified and the entire cosmos can be actualized through Torah.</li>
      <li><strong>§7:</strong> Immersion in the <em>mikveh</em> is the mechanism for this return. Sin causes the holy life-force to retreat from actuality back into potential. The <em>mikveh</em> — where the spirit of Mashiach hovers — is the original state of potential, where all was contained in water before creation. Immersion means returning to that root, then re-emerging with renewed holy vitality. The graves of tzadikim function similarly — drawing <em>pi shnayim</em> from ever-higher levels even after the tzadik's passing.</li>
      <li><strong>§8:</strong> Multiple scriptural appearances of doubled divine language (<em>Anochi Anochi, Ani Ani, Hashem Hashem</em>) all encode <em>pi shnayim</em>: the second always surpasses the first in perfection and mercy. Shabbos's double bread and Shavuos's two loaves share this structure.</li>
      <li><strong>§9:</strong> <em>Shnayim mikra v'echad targum</em> — reading each Torah portion twice and the Aramaic Targum once — mirrors this: the "double Scripture" provides the <em>pi shnayim</em> illumination needed to rectify the Tree of Knowledge (represented by the Targum, the zone of mixed good and evil).</li>
      <li><strong>§10–11:</strong> Chavakuk's prayer and the Thirteen Attributes both encode <em>pi shnayim</em>. The second <em>Hashem</em> in the Thirteen Attributes is, per the Zohar, "more complete" than the first — because after sin one needs a higher grade of mercy than sufficed before. This is only available through the great tzadikim who continually ascend.</li>
      <li><strong>§12:</strong> Full circle: the <em>mikveh</em> restores the actualized state lost through sin, by returning one to the primordial waters where G-d's spirit (= spirit of Mashiach) hovers — the womb of all potential — and re-emerging purified. This is the ultimate <em>tikkun</em> of teshuvah.</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:10px; font-style:italic; color:#2d5a2d;">
      <strong>Note from translator:</strong> The phrase <em>chayehu</em> in §10 ("revive it") and the related <em>Targum</em> / Tree of Knowledge material in §9 contain layers of allusion to Kabbalistic texts that a fuller commentary would expand upon. Readers are encouraged to study the original <em>Likutay Moharan</em> I:66 and I:19 alongside this halacha.
    </p>
  </div>

</div>

<hr class="section-divider"><!-- TRANSLATOR'S SUMMARY BOX -->
  <div class="summary-box">
    <div class="summary-title">🖋 Translator's Summary — Hechsher Kailim 4, Sections 13–24</div>

    <p><strong>Theme:</strong> This installment deepens the exploration of <em>pi shnayim</em>, vessels, will, and obstacles — weaving together the laws of <em>tevilas kailim</em> (immersion of vessels), the role of the Kohen, the blemish of the <em>bris</em>, the secret of the <em>mikveh</em>'s dependence on the ground, and a magnificent Midrash about Moshe and the Satan at Matan Torah.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>§13:</strong> Non-Jewish eating-vessels are spiritually analogous to "broken cisterns" (Jeremiah 2:13) — they cannot channel the <em>da'as</em>-waters from above. Immersing them in the <em>mikveh</em> restores them to the primordial waters where the spirit of Mashiach hovers, remaking them into holy vessels for drawing vitality.</li>

      <li><strong>§14:</strong> The commandment of <em>tevilas kailim</em> was revealed by Elazar the Kohen precisely when Moshe came to anger — because sweetening anger is the Kohen's work. The Kohen stands alongside the King (= Mashiach) and draws the life-spirit from the spirit of Mashiach into actuality. Aharon was Moshe's first student — and thus the archetype of the student who receives <em>pi shnayim</em>. The Kohen's verse in <em>Vezos HaBracha</em> encodes all of this: <em>chaylo</em> = potential, <em>po'al yadav</em> = actuality, <em>tirtzeh</em> = will/yearning.</li>

      <li><strong>§15:</strong> The proximity of the Midian episode to Moshe's death is not coincidental. The <em>sitra achra</em>'s last assault — seducing Israel through licentiousness — forced Moshe to realize that the rectification could only be completed after his passing. His anger arose from his infinite love for Israel.</li>

      <li><strong>§16:</strong> The blemish of the <em>bris</em> is the severest of all blemishes because during marital union all one's vitality and the vitality of all one's organs is fully engaged — for in holiness this is the channel for drawing the highest souls into the world. The opposing forces mobilize proportionally. Even a permitted union requires immersion, and Ezra's enactment is grounded in this deep principle.</li>

      <li><strong>§17:</strong> Rashi's interpretation of <em>keri</em> (Leviticus 26) as "obstacle" is foundational. All of Israel's exiles, at their root, flow from this blemish. The redemption is through the tzadikim who dwell in the dust — and through transforming obstacles into <em>ratzon</em> (will).</li>

      <li><strong>§18:</strong> The requirement for the <em>mikveh</em> to rest upon the ground is profound: it is the earth — source of all desires, physicality, and obstacles — that is also the womb of will. By humbling oneself as dust and seeing the truth, one generates the will that breaks obstacles and makes vessels. "Truth springs from the earth" (Psalms 85:12). <em>Eretz</em> = <em>ratzon</em>.</li>

      <li><strong>§19:</strong> The Tanna's prayer after Shemoneh Esray encapsulates the entire system: silence before enemies → become dust → see truth → strengthen desire → open the gates → pursue commandments → enemies fall.</li>

      <li><strong>§20:</strong> The <em>mikveh</em> of Shavuos flows from the Fiftieth Gate — the supreme root from which <em>pi shnayim</em> is drawn. Immersion is self-sacrifice: entering the water = surrendering the soul momentarily. This nullification draws a new holy spirit. Rabbi Akiva's praise and Rebbe Nachman's teaching about staying under water are both expressions of this.</li>

      <li><strong>§21:</strong> The waving of the Omer defeats enemies. The forty-nine days of the <em>Omer</em> are forty-nine refinements — "silver refined sevenfold" = 7×7 = 49.</li>

      <li><strong>§22–23:</strong> Jeremiah's prophecy about the end of exile: G-d appears "from afar" — precisely because the people have descended so low that the rectification must come from the highest distance. The great controversy and opposition that surrounds those close to the true tzadik is itself the measure of how high their rectification must come from. Mesiras nefesh breaks through it all.</li>

      <li><strong>§24:</strong> The Midrash of Moshe and the Satan at Matan Torah is given a remarkable interpretation: Moshe was not being deceptive. His message was: the Torah is "with the sea and the deep" — meaning it requires sea-like and depth-like exertion and self-sacrifice to attain. The Satan, taking this literally, ran to the sea and deep in confusion. Moshe's final answer — "it is with the Holy One, blessed be He" — means: its innermost dimension remains with G-d always; it can only be drawn into any person and generation through strong will and self-sacrifice. The section concludes with Deuteronomy 30:12–14: "It is not in heaven… nor beyond the sea" — for the essential thing depends on <em>ratzon</em> in the heart and mouth. The moment one is truly willing to give everything, G-d immediately helps. The seal of G-d is <em>emes</em> (truth) — <span class="heb">אמת</span> — first, middle, and last letter of the alphabet. The one who looks at truth without self-deception will find the will to break all obstacles.</li>
    </ul>

    <p style="margin-top:10px; font-style:italic; color:#2d5a2d;">
      <strong>Note from translator:</strong> In §24 the Midrash source is cited by Rashi to Job 28:14–22. The phrase <em>"ikara d'yitzra bisha"</em> (§15, regarding the essential root of the evil inclination) is left in Aramaic in the original, reflecting the Rebbe's use of Aramaic idiom when citing Kabbalistic sources. The <em>nine chambers</em> reference in §18 alludes to a teaching in <em>Likutay Moharan</em> that requires separate study; the precise cross-reference should be confirmed with a Breslov scholar.
    </p>
  </div>

</div>

<hr class="section-divider"><!-- TRANSLATOR'S SUMMARY BOX -->
  <div class="summary-box">
    <div class="summary-title">🖋 Translator's Summary — Hechsher Kailim 4, Sections 25–38</div>

    <p><strong>Theme:</strong> This final installment draws all the threads of the teaching together into a grand unified vision: the night of Shavuos, the omer-count, Eliyahu and the <em>bris</em>, Yom Kippur and the Kohen Gadol, Rosh Hashana, the Kohen Gadol's prayer, Pinchas/Eliyahu, and the opening verses of Berayshis — all shown to be expressions of a single principle: breaking obstacles through will, bringing from potential into actuality, and drawing the double-portion spirit (<em>pi shnayim</em>) of Mashiach.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>§25:</strong> The custom of reciting the beginning and end of every book on Shavuos night encodes the <em>koach/po'al</em> (potential/actuality) structure. The beginning of a book = potential; the end = actualization. Even the Torah's weekly portions carry this: Berayshis begins at potential, closes at Noach = sustainer of creation. The portio of Noach closes at the birth of Avraham = the one who actualized G-d's presence in the world.</li>

      <li><strong>§26:</strong> Why recite <em>all</em> the books? To recall before G-d the vast goodness already granted — from the five books of Moses through the six orders of Mishnah through the Torah novellae of the true <em>tzadikim</em> of recent generations. This recollection builds the <em>ratzon</em> (will) and hope to receive Torah anew on Shavuos.</li>

      <li><strong>§27:</strong> Psalm 68 ("Let G-d arise") is the prayer for breaking obstacles. The key verse <em>"uzecha Elokim zu pa'alta lanu"</em> — "that which You have wrought for us" — is read: You already brought everything from potential into actuality at Matan Torah; do so again for us now. The merit of Binyamin and Yehudah leaping into the sea is invoked as the archetype of self-sacrifice that breaks obstacles.</li>

      <li><strong>§28:</strong> Psalm 44 ("O G-d, with our own ears we have heard") makes the same argument: You drove out the nations for us (= broke the obstacles); now command salvation for Yaakov (= <em>emes</em>) again. The entire psalm is read as a prayer of the worthy ones of Israel who endure persecution <em>for the sake of truth.</em></li>

      <li><strong>§29:</strong> Psalm 31 ("Let lying lips be struck dumb") is the prayer against opponents of the true <em>tzadik</em>. The verse <em>"how great is Your goodness hidden away for those who fear You — <em>pa'alta</em>"</em> contains the key word <em>pa'alta</em> (You wrought/actualized). The conclusion — <em>"be strong, all who hope to Hashem"</em> — is addressed to <em>kol</em> (everyone), even the most fallen: maintain the <em>ratzon</em> no matter what.</li>

      <li><strong>§30:</strong> The reason for staying awake all night on Shavuos: darkness/night = obstacles. The omer-count has amplified our holy will to overpower the obstacles. Having broken through them, we no longer need sleep (= the nullification that comes from being overwhelmed by obstacles) — we can now engage in Torah all night. The counting of the omer is thus the spiritual preparation that makes the Shavuos night vigil possible.</li>

      <li><strong>§31:</strong> <em>Bris milah</em> and Eliyahu. Eliyahu = Pinchas the Kohen = the one who sweetens wrath and draws the spirit of Mashiach. Circumcision (removing the <em>arlah</em> and the <em>priah</em>) = breaking the two layers of obstacles that cover the holy <em>bris</em>. These two layers correspond precisely to the two-fold (<em>pi shnayim</em>) structure. Torah can only be received through <em>milah</em> and <em>tevilah</em> — both of which are expressions of breaking obstacles and self-sacrifice.</li>

      <li><strong>§32:</strong> The cosmic chain: Rectification of <em>bris</em> → holy births → Yosef (firstborn/pi shnayim) → transmission from father to son, rebbe to student (Eliyahu called "my father" by Elisha) → revelation of divinity from generation to generation. Two husks over the <em>bris</em> (arlah and priah) = two obstacles corresponding to <em>pi shnayim</em>. The cutting = <span class="heb">חת"ך</span> (the gematria of twice <em>ruach</em>, from LM I:66). Isaiah 59:21: G-d's covenant that Torah will never be forgotten = the eternal flowing of <em>pi shnayim</em>.</li>

      <li><strong>§33:</strong> Yom Kippur's reading "After the death of the two sons of Aharon" is the key. The souls of Nadav and Avihu transmitted into Pinchas/Eliyahu = the source of <em>pi shnayim</em>. Yom Kippur = completing the rectification of creation (Rosh Hashana begun, Yom Kippur completed). The Kohen Gadol ascending to <em>lifnai v'lifnim</em> (the innermost sanctuary) = ascending to a supremely lofty level to draw new spirit. Ten Days of Repentance = Ten Utterances of creation = Ten Commandments. <em>Kol Nidray</em> / annulment of vows = opening of regret = the <em>pesach</em> (opening) that draws vitality from above. Ne'ilah prayer = crying for the gate to remain open. The Kohen Gadol's five immersions on Yom Kippur = five repetitions of the mikveh-principle of drawing from the spirit of Mashiach.</li>

      <li><strong>§34:</strong> The Kohen Gadol's three-part prayer (no miscarriages; no dependence on others; kingship of Mashiach not depart from Yehudah) is a single prayer for the completion of rectification: (1) multiplication of Israel = actualizing creation; (2) financial independence = preserving truth (the face changes when dependent on others); (3) Mashiach's kingdom = the spirit of Mashiach through whom all rectification flows.</li>

      <li><strong>§35:</strong> Nadav and Avihu's sin (not marrying) = failing to actualize the holy <em>bris</em> = blemishing <em>koach el hapo'al</em>. Their rectification through being transmitted into Pinchas at the moment of his zealotry = the paradigmatic act of breaking a blemish of the <em>bris</em>. Therefore Yom Kippur's atonement is connected to their death.</li>

      <li><strong>§36:</strong> A brief but weighty appendix: livelihood-obstacles are the primary form taken by the obstacles that prevent Torah-service. By trusting G-d absolutely regarding livelihood and breaking this obstacle — "no man touches what is designated for his fellow" — one earns the toil of Torah. <em>"All man's toil is for his mouth"</em> (Ecclesiastes 6:7) = all obstacles ultimately reduce to livelihood-pressure.</li>

      <li><strong>§37:</strong> Pinchas = Eliyahu. His act of zealotry over the blemish of the <em>bris</em> = sweetening wrath = drawing the spirit of Mashiach = <em>pi shnayim</em>. One sentence — the essential conclusion of the entire Eliyahu-Pinchas theme.</li>

      <li><strong>§38:</strong> Berayshis 1:1–4 decoded through the lens of the entire teaching: "In the beginning..." = creation in potential. <em>Tohu vavohu</em> and darkness = the totality of all obstacles = the four exiles, the last of which is <em>tehom</em> (the depths, as in "deep calls to deep"). "And the spirit of G-d hovering over the waters" = the spirit of Mashiach = the essential rectification and hope. "Let there be light" = distinguishing right from left = actualization. G-d hiding the primordial light for the <em>tzadikim</em> = the light is accessed in proportion to obstacles broken — the hidden light is not lost but earned through <em>menius</em>-breaking. The <em>tzadikim</em> and those who cleave to them already draw from it now.</li>
    </ul>

    <p style="margin-top:12px; font-style:italic; color:#2d5a2d;">
      <strong>Note from translator:</strong> §32 contains a reference to the <em>gematria</em> of <span class="heb">חת"ך</span> = two times <em>ruach</em> (= 2 × 214 = 428; <span class="heb">ח</span>=8, <span class="heb">ת</span>=400, <span class="heb">כ</span>=20 = 428); this derives from the final letters of <span class="heb">פּוֹתֵחַ אֶת יָדֶיךָ</span> as explained at length in <em>Likutay Moharan</em> I:66. §33 references <em>Tzemach Tzadik</em> — a Breslov compilation; the Arizal source on Nadav, Avihu, and Pinchas is primarily in <em>Sha'ar HaGilgulim</em>, Introduction 32. §36's editorial note <em>"shayach l'ayl"</em> (belongs above) appears in the original text, indicating it may have been a marginal addition incorporated into the body.
    </p>
  </div>

</div>

<hr class="section-divider"><!-- TRANSLATOR'S SUMMARY BOX -->
  <div class="summary-box">
    <div class="summary-title">🖋 Translator's Summary — Hechsher Kailim 4, Sections 39–51 (Final)</div>

    <p><strong>Theme:</strong> The final installment weaves together counting and number, the census of Israel, handwashing, the fire-kashering of vessels, Moshe's final prayer, Dovid's yahrzeit, the dispute of Korach — and concludes with a magnificent exposition on the concept of <em>pesach</em> (opening/channel) as the essential mechanism of all spiritual drawing, all holiness, all life, and all death. Tzitzis, mezuza, skin afflictions, and the opening of the earth to swallow Korach are all revealed as aspects of this single principle.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>§39:</strong> G-d's counting of the days of creation encodes the <em>koach/po'al</em> principle. Number (<em>mispar</em>) only becomes applicable once things have moved from potential to actuality. The essential rectification: returning the plurality back to the One without nullifying the world — the <em>ratzo vashuv</em> of Ezekiel's living creatures. The omer-count is the rectification of number before Shavuos — seven weeks corresponding to the seven days of creation — reconnecting all plurality to its root.</li>

      <li><strong>§40:</strong> The portion of <em>Bamidbar</em> (counting of Israel) precedes Shavuos for this reason: a great multitude of students is needed to receive the <em>pi shnayim</em> from the <em>tzadik</em>, since more holy vessels can receive more light. But this requires <em>ahavas chavayrim</em> (love of companions) — otherwise the <em>sitra achra</em> gains hold through the very multiplicity. The half-shekel = the two <em>yuds</em>. Tzedakah draws the opening from above to below. Dispute within Israel is the greatest obstacle of all. Eliyahu's role: to restore the heart of fathers to children = <em>ruach dil'ayla</em> returning to <em>ruach dil'tata</em>. Ten for a <em>minyan</em> = the Yud — the quorum that can draw from upper Yud to lower Yud.</li>

      <li><strong>§41:</strong> Handwashing before meals — the water derives from the mikveh waters where the spirit of Mashiach hovers. The hands are the instruments of <em>koach v'po'al</em> (right and left, potential and actuality). All human activity is accomplished through hands. Therefore impurity and purity are bound to them above all. Handwashing before eating = purifying the primary instrument of actualization before the act of drawing vitality.</li>

      <li><strong>§42:</strong> Handwashing before prayer — especially morning handwashing upon waking. Night/sleep = the darkness of obstacles, which deposit the <em>ruach ra'ah</em> most powerfully on the hands. Morning handwashing purifies the hands to pray — for prayer is speech, and speech is the essential channel of <em>koach el hapo'al</em>. The Talmudic statement "it is as if he immersed" confirms: handwashing = mikveh immersion.</li>

      <li><strong>§43:</strong> Kashering non-Jewish vessels through fire — the halachic process is decoded. Fire-purging = the downfall of the wicked (the forbidden matter is the husk/wickedness; it was absorbed through the fire of desires and jealousy, so it is expelled through fire). The removal of rust before kashering = the removal of falsehood that covers truth. Gold and silver = supernal hues whose root is very high — corrupted when one becomes dependent on others and "changes his face." After fire-kashering (= downfall of wicked = perfection of speech), mikveh immersion draws the vowel-points that complete the letters of speech, and new holy spirit flows.</li>

      <li><strong>§44:</strong> Moshe's prayer for a successor: <em>"Hashem, G-d of the <em>ruchos</em>"</em> — the plural <em>ruchos</em> (spirits) refers to the two spirits of <em>pi shnayim</em>. The essential eternal rectification requires a <em>tzadik</em> to leave students who will continue drawing <em>pi shnayim</em> after his passing.</li>

      <li><strong>§45:</strong> The same formula — <em>"G-d of the spirits"</em> — appears when Moshe and Aharon fall on their faces during Korach's rebellion. The <em>ruchos</em> is the key: even the worst outbreak of dispute and heresy is healed through the few worthy students who draw <em>pi shnayim</em> from the <em>tzadik</em>. Moshe's plea: "one man sinned" — Korach was the instigator; the rest were led astray innocently, and the illumination of <em>pi shnayim</em> through the <em>tzadik</em>'s students will eventually bring them all back to truth.</li>

      <li><strong>§46–47:</strong> Dovid died on Shavuos because his passing is what enables the annual drawing of <em>pi shnayim</em> for kabbalas haTorah — for the <em>tzadik</em> is most powerful after his histalkus. <em>Dovid King of Israel lives and endures</em> = his Torah continues to be drawn = that is his life. Song of Songs 3:4 ("scarcely had I passed by them") = Rashi: after Moshe's passing, the Land was conquered — tzadikim are greater in death than in life.</li>

      <li><strong>§48:</strong> A vessel not of that day cannot render food forbidden — the forbidden flavor is impaired after its time. The wicked have power only on their day. The persecuted must wait, pray, and study — for each morning a new sifting of truth from falsehood occurs (<em>ma'asay Berayshis</em> renewed daily), and this sifting is itself the gradual downfall of the wicked. Shema is fixed in the morning blessings of the luminaries because Shema = truth = the source of the luminaries' light. The stories of Sancheriv and Haman illustrate: the wicked's power was broken the very moment their day passed and the new day began.</li>

      <li><strong>§49–50:</strong> The opening of the earth to swallow Korach — the grand climax. The entire section on the <em>pesach</em> (opening/channel) is the deepest statement of the teaching: light is infinite and always desires to flow; it cannot be received directly; vessels/channels/contractions are needed; but they must not completely conceal the light — rather, a <em>pesach</em> (opening) must remain at each level through which light flows in graduated measure. This applies at every spiritual level — one must always make a new opening at a higher level. <em>"Open for me the gates of righteousness"</em> = the prayer for access to ever higher levels. <em>"This is the gate of Hashem — tzadikim shall enter through it"</em> = only the <em>tzadikim</em> can open these gates. Moshe's miracle: he opened an actual physical opening in the earth — demonstrating that the <em>tzadik</em>'s power extends from the highest of heights to the lowest of earthly depths. Death and Resurrection: without sin, man would have made the opening himself and lived forever; because of sin, the soul must be refined through death, until Resurrection = G-d's opening of the grave. Korach denied the entire concept of the <em>tzadik</em>'s power to open — therefore his punishment: the earth opened its mouth.</li>

      <li><strong>§51:</strong> Three questions of Korach — tzitzis, mezuza, and the bright patch of leprosy — are all aspects of the same heresy. A garment entirely of <em>techeles</em>: denying that you need channels/threads to receive infinite light in measured doses. A house full of books: denying that you need the specific opening of the mezuza. The bright patch: in impurity too there are openings — when the patch spreads over the entire body (<em>parcha b'kulo tahor</em>) the opening is nullified and it is pure. Non-Jews cannot defile through <em>nega'im</em> — because the laws of purity apply only where there is holiness to be protected and openings to be rectified. All commandments and transgressions depend on the opening: <em>"You open Your hands"</em> in holiness; and the dead are those <em>"cut off from Your hand"</em> — they blemished the opening. The entire Halacha ends by circling back to its beginning: Korach denied Moshe; Moshe's power is to make an opening from the height of all heights to the depth of all depths; the earth opened its mouth — and the truth was revealed.</li>
    </ul>

    <p style="margin-top:12px; font-style:italic; color:#2d5a2d;">
      <strong>Note from translator:</strong> §48 contains an extended excursus on daily siftings (<em>bairurim</em>) and the miracle of Sancheriv's downfall, citing Sanhedrin 95a, which belongs to a sub-theme of truth-revelation as the mechanism of the wicked's downfall — connecting the halachic rule of <em>ayno ben yomo</em> to a cosmic principle. §49 is among the longest and most philosophically dense sections in the entire halacha — comparable in depth to the great <em>pesach</em>-passage of <em>Likutay Moharan</em> I:66 itself. The term <em>baheres k'gris</em> (§51) refers to the Mishnah in <em>Nega'im</em> 8:3; the Zoharic phrase <em>d'achlai kula v'shatzi kula</em> (§51) is cited in the name of our master in <em>Likutay Moharan</em> I:49. The colophon <em>chazak chazak v'nischazek</em> is traditional at the conclusion of a book of the Torah; its appearance here marks the completion of Hechsher Kailim 4 and of the entire halacha.
    </p>
